  "body": "Machine files for Tormach's three routers (#620): the **24R**, the **xsTECH Pro** and the plain **xsTECH**.\n\n## Sources (cited in `docs/decisions/620-tormach-routers.md`)\n- **24R:** Tormach UM10564 0826A §2.2, Machine Specifications.\n- **xsTECH Pro:** the xsTECH Pro spec sheet (2026) and UM10859 (xsTECH Pro CE manual) §2.\n\n## What changed\n- **`assets/machines/tormach-24r.mch`:**\n  - X 24.75 / Y 55.75 / Z 6.7 in;\n  - 1.5 kW electrospindle, **10000-24000 rpm**;\n  - 200 IPM on every axis;\n  - ATC left unstated (optional, no station count given).\n- **`assets/machines/tormach-xstech-pro.mch`:**\n  - X 10.2 (with the standard tool changer);\n  - **Y 7.9** (the CE manual) over the US sheet's 9.3, and Z 6.3 over the CE manual's 6.5. Where the documents disagree, the smaller travel is used, since it can only refuse a move, never let a bad one through;\n  - 0.35 kW, max 20000 rpm with no minimum stated;\n  - a 4-station ATC (T5 and up is refused);\n  - **no feed ceiling stated**, because none is documented.\n- **Both machines:**\n  - rigid tapping false (not documented), so a tapping cycle is refused by name;\n  - no coolant (none documented), so a Flood op is refused by name;\n  - safe retract Z0, the Tormach convention.\n- **Registration:** added to `MACHINES`. Both get the Tormach PathPilot mill post, as the Tormach mills do.\n\n## How tested\n- **New `tormach_routers` (4):**\n  - the documents' figures;\n  - the xsTECH Pro states no feed ceiling;\n  - both post through PathPilot (S18000, G20, no warnings);\n  - a **5000 rpm job is refused on the 24R** (\"below the machine's 10000\"), and the same job at 18000 posts.\n- **Gate:** machine 32, machine_library, tormach_machines, tormach_safe_retract, tormach_post, posting and conformance pass. Clippy is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\nA PathPilot Hub run of a profile on a router.\n\n## The plain xsTECH Router (commit e1ff076d)\nTormach told the contributor that no separate manual was made for it (its documentation lives in the knowledge base). Its figures therefore come from Tormach's published spec sheet, product pages and knowledge base, each cited with its URL and retrieval date in the decision file.\n- **Travel:** X 8.6 / Y 6.3 / Z 2.4 in. All sources agree.\n- **Spindle:** 5000-15000 rpm, 0.08 kW, direct drive. The sources disagree (80-125 W, 3000/5000 to 15000/20000 rpm), so the narrowest window and the smallest motor are used: a refusal limit that is too narrow can only refuse a job, never pass a bad one. A 15-20k rpm job on a newer unit is refused by name, and the decision file says how to widen it.\n- **Feed:** 120 IPM on every axis, as both feed and rapid. **Tool change:** manual ER11 collet, no ATC.\n- **Rigid tapping and coolant:** not documented, so both are refused by name.\n- **Left unstated:** a separate rapid rate, tool length and diameter limits, and 4th-axis figures.\n\n**Tests:** `tormach_routers` 5 pass. The xsTECH spec row is new. The PathPilot posting test now covers all three routers (12000 rpm). `the_xstech_spindle_window_is_the_narrowest_stated` checks that 18000 and 4000 rpm are refused and 12000 passes. tormach_machines, machine, machine_library, posting, conformance, tormach_safe_retract and tormach_post pass, and clippy is clean.\n\n**Not exercised:** a Hub run of a router profile.\n\nChangelog: Tormach 24R, xsTECH Pro and xsTECH router machine files (PathPilot post; spindle windows, travels and tool changers from Tormach's published figures, undocumented capabilities refused by name) (#620)\n\nFixes #620\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAgjpiZbXSvmXLvdmL\n\n",
